WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.200
Blayne Weaver: I think I wrote that in like
5 weeks of just getting up and like hammering

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.470
away at it.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:12.499
I’ll tell you one thing that also is a great
lesson in….

00:00:12.499 --> 00:00:19.240
Film Courage: Blayne, your IMDB page shows
12 writing credits (and I’m sure you’ve

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:24.849
written more scripts than are just there),
but of those films which production taught

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:27.260
you the most about writing and why?

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.360
Blayne Weaver: Oh?

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.360
Interesting.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.800
You know…what we talked about a little bit
earlier, the biggest lesson about writing

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.800
it, get up and do it.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.649
It is a job.

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:45.160
I’m the kind of guy that I like a glass
of wine…you know… I like some music going..that’s

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.789
very romantic and I’m sure Hemingway would
be very proud but that’s not like really

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.649
how you get a script done or how you write
that many scripts.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:55.910
And I am always writing.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.210
So it’s like you’ve got to get up, you’ve
got to spend time with your coffee.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.210
You’ve got to look at it.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.711
Sometimes it’s just sitting there not being
able to write anything that gets you to two

00:01:04.711 --> 00:01:10.750
days later to where you can answer the question
and put it on paper.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.979
But I love working on all different genres.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:18.900
That’s what fun for me to do like a punch
up on a kid’s movie or here’s a horror

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.820
film or here’s a family drama.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:28.291
But I love getting to do all of them because
I love movies and it gets boring doing the

00:01:28.291 --> 00:01:29.939
same thing over and over again.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:36.710
So as far as what taught me the most, I think
it was just being thrown the random “Here,

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.409
I need you to do the suspense action film
for me.”

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:43.910
And sometimes you’ll get a finished script
and you’ll fix it.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:49.270
And sometimes you’ll get a paragraph and
you’ll execute it, you know?

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.270
So yeah, I don’t know?

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.270
All of them I guess?

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.909
It’s just the process of writing.

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:56.460
You just get better at it.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:02.039
That’s something I tell young people all
the time is you get better at writing the

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:03.039
more you write.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:04.060
So you have to write.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.130
You know what I mean?

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.130
All the time.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.520
Film Courage: Well I know with MANIC you said
that it showed you that you wanted to direct

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.270
for various reasons.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:18.110
And then with WEATHER GIRL it sounds like
being in an uncomfortable situation really

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.390
pushed you.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.300
How long did that script take, WEATHER GIRL?

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.510
Blayne Weaver: That script almost wrote itself.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:30.770
When I think of the easiest writing experiences,
I think I probably wrote that in 5 weeks of

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.080
just like getting up and hammering away at
it.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.790
I’ll tell you one thing also that I think
there is a great lesson in directing your

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:47.871
own script because you find in the editing
room, if there are flaws in your script, in

00:02:47.871 --> 00:02:51.350
your logic, in your plot line, you will find
it in editing!

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.370
And it’s crazy to me.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.090
It’s like 6 MONTH RULE, I got almost no
notes on that script.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.350
That script…everybody dug that script.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:02.890
Like it was just one of those things, like
sometimes as a writer, something will resonate.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.730
And people just liked it.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:09.300
They’re like “He talks a little too much
but other than that, like great!”

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:15.020
But then we got into editing and I have all
of these structural problems.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.460
Like, he has this moment where he realizes
he loves the girl twice.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.350
Now why didn’t I see that?

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:24.160
Why didn’t anyone who read the script say
“This moment happens twice?”

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.660
But nobody did, you know?

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.980
And it’s made me look at scripts a lot harder
now, especially my own.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:34.790
Like when is this moment in the movie and
it has to be this special moment and it can’t

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.830
be duplicated because we had to move a lot
of things around.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:45.930
And this great editor Abe Levy who helped
me streamline that story that in a proper

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.720
world should have been streamlined on the
page, you know what I mean?

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.760
Film Courage: So with WEATHER GIRL, how much
do you think you really…[hesitates]…I

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:55.760
don’t know.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.200
I’m just trying to envision that time for
you.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.370
Do you think with some of your other productions
you were more comfortable and it was harder

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.780
to finish it (in terms of the script)?

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:06.910
Blayne Weaver: In terms of the script?

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:14.260
I think…you know…I didn’t have television.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.590
It was really depressing [laughs].

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:22.380
I had this season of THE OFFICE that I would
watch over and over and over again.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:27.160
And so, like I didn't really have anything
to do except sit there and write, which was

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.160
by design.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.430
I didn’t get cable (television).

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.150
I just wanted to work.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:40.510
And it was actually easier than to write then
say when you’re…happy with a girlfriend,

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:41.510
you know?

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:45.290
That’s the hardest time for me to write,
when I’m happy and busy.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:46.670
I just flew back from New York.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.470
And I loved living in New York because there
is so much to do.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.960
It’s hard for me to write in New York.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.160
Because I’m always distracted by things
that are going on outside.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.850
“What’s my roommate doing down there?

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:58.850
It sounds like he’s having fun.

00:04:58.850 --> 00:04:59.850
Are you drinking a beer?

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.760
Let me have some beer.”

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.070
So sometimes that lonely time is actually
easier.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:11.170
Film Courage: So from being a bartender, I
know you are probably busy making drinks and

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:15.080
getting customers’ orders, but you probably
had some moments where you could see the Los

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.930
Angeles crowd from this other perspective.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.060
How much did you see that from some of the
patrons that went there [to his bar]?

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.900
Even though you don’t really know their
personal lives and everybody is putting on

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:25.900
this happy facade.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:30.620
Blayne Weaver: I needed to get out of Los
Angeles after 6 MONTH RULE.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:36.520
After 6 MONTH RULE I kind of hit this…I
was super saturated with Los Angeles.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.330
You know I’d been here a really long time
since I was 19-years-old.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.760
[I was a] kid actor, went to UCLA, you know?

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.990
I’d been in this town for a long time and
I just needed out because of what I think

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:49.990
you’re saying.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.420
Because I saw the stereotypes.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.430
I saw the “LA shallowness” or whatever.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.170
And I saw it in me, you know?

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.840
Which was not good either.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.970
And it was not what I wanted.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:07.950
So I fled to New York and enjoyed my time
very much there and I’ve just moved back

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.650
here for business reasons.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:11.780
And LA is different to me now.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.720
I have perspective.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:21.900
It’s not the stereotypes that I was seeing
when I was here before.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.681
I was painting my own unhappy picture of it.

00:06:25.681 --> 00:06:27.420
It’s actually not that bad a place.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.990
I think I’d just been here too long [laughs].

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.660
Film Courage: And I’ve heard the same thing
from people who’ve been in New York [City]

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:32.660
having come here [to Los Angeles].

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:33.660
Blayne Weaver: Oh absolutely!

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:34.660
Film Courage: So I think it’s just a change
of atmosphere.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:38.740
Blayne Weaver: I know people who have lived
in New York much longer than I have and I’ll

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.610
tell them how much I love it and they’ll
be like “Try 5 more years!”

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.270
Blayne Weaver: Well maybe you should give
it a break?

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.800
Maybe you should go somewhere else?

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:46.800
That’s what I did.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.801
Film Courage: But you are also coming here
[to Los Angeles] with a film.

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:49.801
Blayne Weaver: Yes.

00:06:49.801 --> 00:06:52.300
Film Courage: A film coming out that has distribution
so I’m sure you’ve got to feel great?

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.639
Blayne Weaver: It’s awesome!

00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:56.290
Film Courage: You’re coming from a different
situation.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.110
Blayne Weaver: Yeah.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:03.740
I mean the thing about…this movie business
is really weird.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:09.950
It’s really weird because it has so many
different levels.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:13.510
You start, you’ve got the script, then you’ve
got to try to raise the money, and that’s

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:14.510
a whole thing.

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:17.770
Then you get the money and you have to put
it together and then that’s a whole thing.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:18.980
I love being on set.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.660
Being on set is my absolute favorite.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:24.040
I’m the best me when I’m on set.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:28.130
And then you wrap and go into post production
which I am not my best me but that is just

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.130
a part of it.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:31.830
And then you have to get the distribution
and then you get to do the film festivals

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.650
and now you’re here.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.760
Literally I will get a link later today for
the pre-sale of [Blayne’s new movie] CUT

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:38.760
TO THE CHASE.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.870
And in 3 weeks [from the original taping of
this interview] it will be out and everybody

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.139
in America will be able to access it.

00:07:44.139 --> 00:07:46.640
And that is kind of like the end, really.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.990
We will come out on DVD in June and that will
be a whole other thing we’ll have to deal

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:51.990
with.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:58.199
But for all practical purposes this journey
is ending next month and like…I’m just

00:07:58.199 --> 00:08:03.860
really excited for…not for it to be over,
but for this pinnacle for like “Here we

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:07.090
are, we made it to the crest.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:08.090
Good job everybody!

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.090
What is next?”

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:09.280
Question for the Viewers: Do you treat your
writing like a job?

